WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.440 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A designer wants a butterfly decoration with matching wings
on either side of a fold.

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:10.720 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The two wings have the same shape, but sliding one sideways
does not make them face each other.

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:13.480 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
What special movement creates a mirror partner?

00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:17.780 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Let us investigate with folds, points and carefully checked
distances.

00:00:17.780 --> 00:00:18.260 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Today we are learning to reflect a shape using equal
perpendicular distances from a mirror line.

00:00:18.260 --> 00:00:29.471 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
We will build an explanation using models, rather than
memorising a rule without understanding it.

00:00:29.471 --> 00:00:35.111 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Our learning goal is to reflect a shape using equal
perpendicular distances from a mirror line.

00:00:35.111 --> 00:00:40.749 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
As you investigate, connect what you can see, what you can
make, and the mathematical language you use.

00:00:40.749 --> 00:00:43.349 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
First, I can identify a mirror line.

00:00:43.349 --> 00:00:46.509 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Second, I can place matching reflected points.

00:00:46.509 --> 00:00:50.269 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Third, I can explain how a reflection differs from a slide.

00:00:50.269 --> 00:00:53.746 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Keep these goals in mind as you explain each discovery.

00:00:53.746 --> 00:00:56.866 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Fold a sheet of paper, place a small ink dot near the fold
and imagine pressing it onto the other half.

00:00:56.866 --> 00:01:04.266 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The matching marks lie equally far from the fold on opposite
sides.

00:01:04.266 --> 00:01:09.365 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
When the paper opens, the crease acts as a mirror line that
connects the two positions.

00:01:09.365 --> 00:01:13.845 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
On our grid, the mirror line is vertical at horizontal
coordinate four.

00:01:13.845 --> 00:01:18.325 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Every point on this line has horizontal coordinate four,
whatever its height.

00:01:18.325 --> 00:01:21.565 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The line is not itself a side of the triangle.

00:01:21.565 --> 00:01:26.417 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
It is the reference that tells us where each matching
reflected point must go.

00:01:26.417 --> 00:01:30.417 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
For this vertical mirror, measure horizontally from the
point to the line.

00:01:30.417 --> 00:01:33.537 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
That direction meets the line at a right angle.

00:01:33.537 --> 00:01:35.857 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
An angled path to some other place on the line is longer and
would not give the correct matching position.

00:01:35.857 --> 00:01:42.953 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The reflected point keeps the same vertical coordinate.

00:01:42.953 --> 00:01:45.033 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Take the vertex at one, one.

00:01:45.033 --> 00:01:48.113 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
It is three units left of the mirror line at four.

00:01:48.113 --> 00:01:52.873 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Its reflected partner must be three units right of that
line, at seven, one.

00:01:52.873 --> 00:01:55.633 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Compare the two horizontal distances.

00:01:55.633 --> 00:02:00.047 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
They are equal, and the joining segment crosses the mirror
at a right angle.

00:02:00.047 --> 00:02:02.527 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The vertex three, one is one unit left of the line, so it
reflects to five, one.

00:02:02.527 --> 00:02:09.167 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The vertex two, three is two units left, so it reflects to
six, three.

00:02:09.167 --> 00:02:11.327 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Join the three matching points in their original order.

00:02:11.327 --> 00:02:16.875 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Their positions create the mirror image of the entire
triangle.

00:02:16.875 --> 00:02:21.995 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The reflected triangle has the same side lengths and angles
as the original.

00:02:21.995 --> 00:02:25.475 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Its facing direction is reversed across the mirror line.

00:02:25.475 --> 00:02:28.155 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
It has not simply been slid to a new place.

00:02:28.155 --> 00:02:34.324 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Corresponding points can travel different distances,
depending on how far each one started from the mirror.

00:02:34.324 --> 00:02:38.563 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Focus on the vertex at two, three and the vertical mirror at
four.

00:02:38.563 --> 00:02:45.603 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Before its reflected partner appears, decide how far it is
from the line and which side the partner must occupy.

00:02:45.603 --> 00:02:51.574 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Predict the full coordinate pair, including the height, and
prepare an equal-distance explanation.

00:02:56.574 --> 00:02:59.054 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Two, three is two units left of the mirror.

00:02:59.054 --> 00:03:00.294 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Moving two units to the right of the mirror reaches six,
three.

00:03:00.294 --> 00:03:00.814 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The height remains three.

00:03:00.814 --> 00:03:11.430 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The total separation between this point and its partner is
four units, but each is only two units from the mirror line
itself.

00:03:11.430 --> 00:03:17.630 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A common mistake is reflecting across the vertical axis even
though the given mirror line is at four.

00:03:17.630 --> 00:03:21.550 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Another is moving every vertex the same sideways distance.

00:03:21.550 --> 00:03:26.870 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Instead, use the specified mirror and measure each point’s
own perpendicular distance.

00:03:26.870 --> 00:03:30.918 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A reflection is controlled by the line, not by one shared
slide.

00:03:30.918 --> 00:03:34.758 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Now use a horizontal mirror at vertical coordinate four.

00:03:34.758 --> 00:03:36.838 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Measure vertically to this line.

00:03:36.838 --> 00:03:44.358 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The horizontal coordinate stays unchanged because each point
and its partner sit directly above and below one another.

00:03:44.358 --> 00:03:49.746 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The equal-distance reasoning is the same, but the direction
in which we count has changed.

00:03:49.746 --> 00:03:52.106 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The vertex two, one is three units below the mirror, so its
partner is two, seven.

00:03:52.106 --> 00:03:54.426 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The vertex four, one reflects to four, seven.

00:03:54.426 --> 00:04:02.186 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The vertex three, three is one unit below the mirror and
reflects to three, five.

00:04:02.186 --> 00:04:06.399 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Check each pair against the line instead of swapping the
coordinate values.

00:04:06.399 --> 00:04:11.719 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Reflecting in a horizontal line does not exchange the two
coordinate positions.

00:04:11.719 --> 00:04:18.399 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Each horizontal value remains what it was; the vertical
position changes according to distance from the mirror.

00:04:18.399 --> 00:04:21.639 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Reading an ordered pair correctly is still important.

00:04:21.639 --> 00:04:27.308 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A reflection rule does not replace the horizontal-first
convention for describing positions.

00:04:27.308 --> 00:04:30.748 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Consider a point at four, two on our vertical mirror line.

00:04:30.748 --> 00:04:32.988 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Where is its reflected partner?

00:04:32.988 --> 00:04:37.908 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Does it need to move to the other side even though its
distance from the mirror is zero?

00:04:37.908 --> 00:04:42.612 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Think about a paper dot placed exactly on a crease, then
predict the position.

00:04:47.612 --> 00:04:49.692 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The point remains at four, two.

00:04:49.692 --> 00:04:56.092 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Its perpendicular distance from the mirror is zero, so the
matching distance on the other side is also zero.

00:04:56.092 --> 00:04:59.332 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Points on the mirror line are fixed during the reflection.

00:04:59.332 --> 00:05:04.385 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Other vertices of the same shape may move while that
particular vertex stays in place.

00:05:04.385 --> 00:05:08.425 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Reflect the reflected triangle across the same line again.

00:05:08.425 --> 00:05:15.265 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Each partner returns to the original position, because it
travels back to the matching distance on the opposite side.

00:05:15.265 --> 00:05:23.537 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
If a second reflection does not restore your starting
diagram, check the line, the perpendicular direction and
your counting of grid intervals.

00:05:23.537 --> 00:05:25.257 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A shape can be reflected to a new position even if it has no
line of symmetry.

00:05:25.257 --> 00:05:34.737 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A line of symmetry is a special mirror line where the
reflected shape matches the original whole outline.

00:05:34.737 --> 00:05:41.454 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Distinguish creating a mirror image from claiming that a
particular object is symmetrical about every line.

00:05:41.454 --> 00:05:45.174 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Draw a short outline on one side of a vertical grid line.

00:05:45.174 --> 00:05:50.054 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Mark its vertices, count each distance to the line and plot
the matching points.

00:05:50.054 --> 00:05:52.694 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Join those partners to make the other wing.

00:05:52.694 --> 00:05:59.157 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Explain how the equal-distance method controls the design
more reliably than copying by eye alone.

00:05:59.157 --> 00:06:05.957 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
To check a proposed reflection, compare corresponding side
lengths and inspect the midpoint of each joining segment.

00:06:05.957 --> 00:06:10.557 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
That midpoint should lie on the mirror line, with the
segment perpendicular to it.

00:06:10.557 --> 00:06:17.096 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
You can describe these ideas using counting and right
angles, without needing an advanced algebraic rule.

00:06:17.096 --> 00:06:18.256 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
You can now locate a mirror line, reflect vertices and
justify matching positions using equal perpendicular
distances.

00:06:18.256 --> 00:06:29.656 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A reflection preserves size while reversing the facing
relationship across the mirror.

00:06:29.656 --> 00:06:36.512 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Choose one reflected pair and explain why its coordinates
are correct, then challenge a partner with a different
mirror line.
